        ":question/id": "c7102fb2-1b14-588a-bd41-c065850a33f5",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-70412",
        ":question/problem": "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}$, $b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $c=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}.$ Find $3a-(b+4c).$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3a-(b+4c) & =3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}-(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -21\\end{bmatrix}-(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ -24\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}12 \\\\ -21\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}18 \\\\ -20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
      {
        ":db/id": 17592186234273,
        ":question/difficulty": {
